WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.567
Book Two, Proposition Fourteen.

2
00:00:02.567 --> 00:00:06.584
To construct a square equal to a given rectilineal figure.

3
00:00:06.584 --> 00:00:12.610
Let A be the given rectilineal figure; it is required to construct a square equal to A.

4
00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:21.007
By One Forty-Five, construct the rectangular parallelogram B D E C equal to the rectilineal figure A.

5
00:00:21.007 --> 00:00:31.670
If B E equals E D, B D E C is already a square; if not, let B E be greater, extend B E to F, and cut off E F equal to E D by One Three.

6
00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:33.803
Bisect B F at G by One Ten.

7
00:00:33.803 --> 00:00:43.360
With center G and radius G B or G F, describe the semicircle B H F; extend D E to meet the semicircle at H, and join G H.

8
00:00:43.780 --> 00:01:01.563
Because the segment B F is cut into equal segments at G and unequal segments at E, by Two Five the rectangle contained by B E and E F together with the square on E G equals the square on G F, and therefore equals the square on radius G H.

9
00:01:01.563 --> 00:01:10.977
By the Pythagorean Theorem, One Forty-Seven, on right triangle G E H, the squares on E H and E G also equal the square on G H.

10
00:01:10.977 --> 00:01:19.196
Subtracting the common square on E G leaves the rectangle contained by B E and E F equal to the square on E H.

11
00:01:19.196 --> 00:01:26.818
Since E F equals E D, the rectangle contained by B E and E F is the rectangle B D E C, which equals A.

12
00:01:26.818 --> 00:01:32.571
Therefore, the square described on E H equals the given rectilineal figure A.

13
00:01:32.571 --> 00:01:34.140
Which was to be done.

14
00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:49.215
Proposition Two Fourteen crowns Book Two, completing the quadrature of any polygon and extracting the geometric mean—x squared equals a b—entirely through Book One and Two area transformations, before Book Six introduces proportions.

15
00:01:49.215 --> 00:01:58.272
As Heath notes, Aristotle cited this very construction in De Anima as discovering the cause of squaring an oblong through the mean proportional.

16
00:01:58.272 --> 00:02:10.600
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G ninety-seven unites twelve direct rules across sixty-four ancestor nodes, fusing One Forty-Five, One Forty-Seven, and Two Five into a single constructive algorithm.

17
00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:24.173
